>
> I tend to view the quest artifacts in terms of what they do for the 
> characters who actually quest for them.  

This isn't a view based on reality though, since the Master Key of
Thievery is not restricted to Rogues only.  Arguments not based in
reality are meaningless.  Arguments based on a specific set of artificial 
self-restrictions on your view are _also_ meaningless.  That said:

Just because _you_ can't complete the Rogue quest without already having
TC doesn't mean that it's not possible and done frequently; thus, the 
teleport control obtained is valuable.  (if nothing else, with an
external source of TC, you can gratuitously eat tengu so that if you
later lose the Key somehow, you still have TC.)

A Rogue has no way short of a wish to guarantee obtaining "detect
monster" spellbooks; thus, warning is valuable.

Half physical damage is always valuable.

> Restricting HPD to rogues would fix this nicely.

That won't be happening.  I don't really like putting artificial 
restrictions on Quest Artifacts in the first place; if other roles 
having a particular benefit from some QA is a problem, then either the 
QA itself is a problem (Eye of Aethiopica) or QAs being 
wishable/boneable at _all_ is a problem.

> Also in Spork there is only one end game beast that hits harder 
> physically than the various magical attacks.

Half physical and half spell damage are not mutually exclusive.

In Spork there are a number of critters who hit harder physically than
they did in vanilla.  In vanilla, half physical damage is good.  In 
Spork, half damage is _better_ because you can't just wedge yourself up
to 600+ HPs pre-Gehennom.

Finally, the endgame is not the only place you'll have to worry about
physical attacks which do damage.

